WEBVTT
TITLE Bit Fields in C: widths, allocation units, and the six things the standard leaves open — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
Three ordinary unsigned members. Each one takes four bytes, whatever value it holds.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
Hours run to 23 and minutes to 59, so most of those thirty-two bits are always zero.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
The same three values as bit-fields: 5 + 6 + 6 = 17 bits, inside one unit, so four bytes.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
Twelve bytes became four, and nothing was lost: the values still fit. But a field is not an int.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
A bit-field is a member name, a colon, and a width in bits: kind : 3.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
The type must be _Bool, signed int, unsigned int, or a type the implementation allows.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
The width may not exceed the bits in that type: 33 for an unsigned int is a constraint violation.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
A bit-field is a named run of bits in a unit. It has no address of its own.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
One allocation unit: thirty-two numbered bits, bit 31 on the left and bit 0 on the right.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
Bit b sits in byte b/8, at position b mod 8. Nothing is stored per field.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
The first field goes into the low-order bits, and the next continues where it stopped.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
Which end is first is implementation-defined. This page draws the choice GCC makes on x86-64.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
b1 is five bits, so it takes bits 0 to 4 of the unit.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
The unnamed field takes the next eleven bits and gives them to nobody.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
b2 takes bits 16 to 21 and b3 takes 22 and 23. Bits 24 to 31 are left unused.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
Widths sum to 24 bits, which is three bytes, but sizeof is four: the unit is rounded up.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
A twenty-bit field leaves twelve bits of the unit free.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
b needs twenty bits and only twelve remain, so b begins a new unit.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
A twenty-eight-bit field leaves four free bits, and an eight-bit field still does not fit.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
So the second field starts at bit 32, not at bit 28. Straddling is implementation-defined.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
Here b1 is followed by a colon and a zero. That is a zero-width field, and it takes no bits.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
It ends the unit. b2 and b3 start in a new unit, so the struct is eight bytes.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
Delete the colon and the zero, change nothing else, and all three fields fit in one unit.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
Eight bytes against four, from one zero. That is the sharpest example of layout as a declaration.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
An unnamed field of width four takes four bits of the unit and gives them to nobody.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
b follows it inside the same unit, at bits 8 and 9.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
The zero-width field after b is a different instruction: it ends the unit, so c starts a new one.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
Padding fields exist to place a field at a chosen bit, usually to match a format someone else defined.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
Two one-bit fields at bits 0 and 1, and then an ordinary int member.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
The int starts at offset 4, which ends the unit above it.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
Four more fields pack into a third unit: ac, four bits of padding, and clock.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
The zero-width field forces a fourth unit for flag, so the whole struct is sixteen bytes.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
Three bits hold eight patterns. Read as unsigned, they are 0 to 7.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
Read as signed, the top bit is the sign, so the same eight patterns are 0 to 3 and then -4 to -1.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
A store is reduced modulo eight: 9 becomes 1, and incrementing 7 gives 0.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
For a plain int field, whether it is signed or unsigned is implementation-defined.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
Taking the address of a field is a constraint violation: the operand of & may not be a bit-field.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
sizeof of a field is not allowed either: sizeof takes an object or a type, and a field is neither.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
There are no arrays of bit-fields, and offsetof on one has no defined answer.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
All four fail for one reason: a field may start mid-byte, so no byte address designates it.

41
00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:07.500
Six properties are left open: the order inside a unit, and whether a field may straddle it.

42
00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:15.000
Also open: the signedness of a plain int field, and which other types are allowed at all.

43
00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:22.500
So a question that asks for sizeof, or for the bits a field occupies, must name the platform.

44
00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:30.000
What is fixed: a following field packs in if it fits, and the width sets the range.

45
00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:37.500
Step one: find the colon. Step two: read the declared type, which fixes the unit and the range.

46
00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:45.000
Step three: pack in order, and start a new unit when a field does not fit. Step four: handle the unnamed forms.

47
00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:52.500
Step five: round the total up to the unit size, then to the alignment of the whole struct.

48
00:05:52.500 --> 00:06:00.000
Two worked lines: 5 + 6 + 15 fits one unit, and the same fields with a zero-width field need two.

